      "If the source is a document, record the page or field; if the source is a system, record the record identifier and extraction time. Keep the identity crosswalk in the approved system and restrict it to the review owner. Keep the original evidence reference next to the coded value so the reviewer can verify the classification. Keep the original request, the approval, and the destination value linked to the same key. If the case moved between owners, include both owner periods in the review note and name the handoff time.\n\nUse a pseudonymous case key and keep the identity crosswalk in an approved system with restricted access. Name the required fields for each article type and mark conditional fields in the dictionary. Record the data extraction date and the version of the cost model used for the population count. Archive the dictionary with the study so a later reviewer can reproduce the categories. Keep the review note with the case so the next sampler does not have to reconstruct the decision from memory.\n\nIf the source is a document, record the page or field; if the source is a system, record the record identifier and extraction time. Give every case a study key that is not the client name, member number, or candidate email. Store sensitive evidence in the approved repository and keep only the reference in the analysis file. Pilot the dictionary with two reviewers and compare their classifications before the main sample. When a value is missing, record the missing state and the reason instead of leaving an empty field in the extract.",
      "Sampling and collection",
      "Stop expanding the sample only when the review question is answered, not when the result starts to look favorable. Use the same collection form for every case and keep the original record linked to the extracted fields. A sample of recent closures can miss the oldest unresolved work, which is often the most informative. A case that is difficult to access is still part of the frame; report the access failure rather than replacing it silently. At Offshore Outsourcing Company, the shift handover review should end with a dated note and a named owner for the next check.\n\nA convenience sample of recently closed cases will overrepresent easy work and underrepresent the tail that needs attention. Collect the exception reason and the action taken before the close so the analysis can separate detection from resolution. Stratify by risk, channel, and owner so the sample is not dominated by the easiest queue. Include at least one case from each owner in scope when the frame allows it. If the evidence is sensitive, record the approved reference and access path rather than copying the content.\n\nSample across the full observation window, including the days when the queue was busiest and the days when coverage changed. Collect the case from the shift handover log, the quality sample, and the approval record, and stop at the verified close. Include reopened, unresolved, and exception cases in the sample frame. A case that is difficult to access is still part of the frame; report the access failure rather than replacing it silently. The note should distinguish what was observed, what was inferred, and what still needs an owner decision.",
      "Analysis and reporting",
      "A difference between two periods is a signal to inspect, not proof that a process change caused the change. Use case-level review to explain an outlier before treating it as a process signal. Keep the case-level table available for review and summarize only the measures that answer the question. When a measure is based on a small sample, state the case count and avoid ranking owners by the result. Record this decision in the study log with the case key, the review date, and the person who approved the classification.\n\nKeep case-level detail available for review, because a summary chart without records cannot support a decision. Report denominators with every rate and separate missing evidence from negative evidence. Record the sample date, the denominator, the numerator, and the exclusion count for every reported rate. When a rate changes, check whether the case mix or the evidence standard changed at the same time. Keep the evidence reference beside the coded value so a reviewer can test the same case without rebuilding the queue.",
      "Limitations",
      "Changes in definitions, staffing, or the shift handover log during the window can create a trend that is not a process effect. Small samples are unstable; report the range and the cases that create the variation. Report the limits of the shift handover log data, including fields that were unavailable or changed during the window. State what a follow-up study would measure and which population it would include. If the source changes after the observation, preserve the earlier value and add a dated note instead of replacing it.\n\nChanges in definitions, staffing, or the quality sampling tool during the window can create a trend that is not a process effect. The record may be incomplete or delayed, so the method cannot infer work that never entered the queue. If the queue changed during the window, explain how the change affects the denominator and the interpretation. State that the result describes the observed sample, not the whole industry or every team in the service. At Offshore Outsourcing Company, the reviewer should be able to explain this step to team lead from the case record alone.",
      "Review and next action",
      "If the action affects single-point coverage, route it to vendor manager and record the approval before implementation. Keep the review note with the shift handover evidence so the next owner can see why the process changed. If the action requires a policy or access change, route it to the owner and record the decision. Keep the prior procedure available so the next reviewer can compare the old and new states. Use the same note in the case file and the study extract; do not create a second version in a personal document.\n\nReport what moved, what did not, and which result is still unexplained. If the action changes stale cost model, record the approval from offshore program owner before implementation. Link the action to the specific shift handover cases that motivated it. Link the action to the case evidence that prompted it, not only to the meeting notes. When a case needs an exception, record the reason, the owner, and the date before continuing the review."
