      "Sampling and collection",
      "Sample across the full observation window, including the days when the queue was busiest and the days when coverage changed. Collect the exception reason and the action taken before the close so the analysis can separate detection from resolution. A sample of recent closures can miss the oldest unresolved work, which is often the most informative. Record the sampling frame as an export with a date, filter, and row count. At Offshore Outsourcing Company, the reviewer should be able to explain this step to offshore program owner from the case record alone.\n\nSample across the full observation window, including the days when the queue was busiest and the days when coverage changed. A second reviewer should re-extract a small subsample and record disagreements before the full analysis. Stratify by risk, channel, and owner so the sample is not dominated by the easiest queue. Include at least one case from each owner in scope when the frame allows it. Use the same note in the case file and the study extract; do not create a second version in a personal document.\n\nA convenience sample of recently closed cases will overrepresent easy work and underrepresent the tail that needs attention. Do not clean the data by filling missing values; preserve the missing state and report it as a result. If a stratum has fewer cases than planned, report the smaller count instead of replacing it with a convenience case. Include at least one case from each owner in scope when the frame allows it. When a case needs an exception, record the reason, the owner, and the date before continuing the review.",
      "Analysis and reporting",
